<node id="Big Tech Q1 2025 comparative valuation and performance table (AAPL, MSFT, NVDA, AMZN, GOOGL) (table)">
  <data key="d0">Big Tech Q1 2025 comparative valuation and performance table (AAPL, MSFT, NVDA, AMZN, GOOGL) (table)</data>
  <data key="d1">table</data>
  <data key="d2">This table compares AAPL ($178.20; $2.75T; 28.4x P/E), MSFT ($415.50; $3.09T; 35.1x P/E), NVDA ($875.40; $2.16T; 68.2x P/E), AMZN ($185.10; $1.93T; 42.7x P/E), and GOOGL ($162.30; $2.01T; 24.8x P/E) using YTD returns and dividend yields. NVDA leads with +82.5% YTD amid the highest P/E, aligning with its AI chip leadership described nearby.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-457105381d6d1e90896cc67920954bd2</data>
  <data key="d4">3</data>
  <data key="d5">1781149693</data>
</node>
<node id="NVIDIA Quarterly Revenue Growth Line Chart (image)">
  <data key="d0">NVIDIA Quarterly Revenue Growth Line Chart (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">A green line chart showing NVIDIA quarterly revenue rising sharply from mid-2023 to Q3 FY24, with a flat period followed by strong growth. The chart appears in the NVIDIA section of a U.S. stock market report and visually supports the report’s claim that AI and data center GPU demand drove major revenue expansion.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c5b7a3a78ae0c5449cefd91a18ec0706</data>
  <data key="d4">3</data>
  <data key="d5">1781149693</data>
</node>
<node id="NVIDIA Q3 FY2024 Revenue Breakdown by Segment (Data Center, Gaming, Professional, Automotive) (table)">
  <data key="d0">NVIDIA Q3 FY2024 Revenue Breakdown by Segment (Data Center, Gaming, Professional, Automotive) (table)</data>
  <data key="d1">table</data>
  <data key="d2">Breaks NVIDIA’s Q3 FY2024 revenue into four segments with $14.51B from Data Center (+279% YoY) representing 80% of total. Gaming contributes $2.86B (+56%, 16%), Professional $0.46B (+105%, 3%), and Automotive $0.26B (+15%, 1%). The table reinforces the report’s AI-chip leadership narrative by tying the largest and fastest growth to Data Center demand.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2ba2789f84f7d59e3d154023f162f859</data>
  <data key="d4">3</data>
  <data key="d5">1781149693</data>
</node>
<node id="Apple Services-Led Financial Metrics (FY2022–FY2024 Est) — Table 1 (table)">
  <data key="d0">Apple Services-Led Financial Metrics (FY2022–FY2024 Est) — Table 1 (table)</data>
  <data key="d1">table</data>
  <data key="d2">Table 1 shows FY2022–FY2024 (Est) financial metrics consistent with Apple’s Services growth thesis: Services Revenue rises from $78.1B to $96.2B while Gross Margin climbs from 43.3% to 45.2%. Total Revenue dips in FY2023 ($394.3B → $383.3B) before rebounding to $400.1B in FY2024 (Est). EPS (Diluted) stays nearly flat ($6.11 → $6.13) then increases to $6.57, and Free Cash Flow falls to $99.6B in FY2023 before improving to $108.2B.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fcbf145ad98382d5c5853a66d59a1789</data>
  <data key="d4">3</data>
  <data key="d5">1781149693</data>
</node>
<node id="Microsoft Azure quarterly revenue growth chart (image)">
  <data key="d0">Microsoft Azure quarterly revenue growth chart (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">A bar chart showing Microsoft Azure year-over-year revenue growth across six quarters, declining from 35% to 26% before rebounding to 29% and 28%. The chart supports the Microsoft cloud and AI monetization discussion in the surrounding Big Tech sector report.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8d65454ffb4dbacac06d7c81dc9c0057</data>
  <data key="d4">3</data>
  <data key="d5">1781149693</data>
</node>
<node id="Analyst consensus ratings and 12-month price targets for AAPL, MSFT, NVDA, AMZN, and GOOGL (table)">
  <data key="d0">Analyst consensus ratings and 12-month price targets for AAPL, MSFT, NVDA, AMZN, and GOOGL (table)</data>
  <data key="d1">table</data>
  <data key="d2">This table compiles consensus “Rating”, “Target Price”, “Upside”, and “Key Catalyst” for AAPL ($210, +17.8%), MSFT ($480, +15.5%), NVDA ($950, +8.5%), AMZN ($220, +18.8%), and GOOGL ($170, +4.7%). The highest upside is AMZN (+18.8%) and the lowest is GOOGL (+4.7%, Hold). Each catalyst aligns with the surrounding narrative: AAPL’s Vision Pro/Services, MSFT’s Azure AI/Copilot, and NVDA’s Blackwell GPU ramp.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6e794b14c77feffc3b4edacbb21c46e8</data>
  <data key="d4">3</data>
  <data key="d5">1781149693</data>
